---
import IonCannonEncounter from "../../../components/lab/IonCannonEncounter.svelte";
import IonCannonFitting from "../../../components/studies/IonCannonFitting.svelte";
import IonCannonFlight from "../../../components/studies/IonCannonFlight.svelte";
import IonCannonTiming from "../../../components/studies/IonCannonTiming.svelte";
import { studies } from "../../../data/studies";
import StudyLayout from "../../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";

const study = studies.find(
  (entry) => entry.slug === "light-ion-cannon-2026-11-08",
);
---

<StudyLayout study={study}>
  <aside aria-label="Study outcome">
    <h2>Completed / Design and implementation scope accepted</h2>
    <p>
      Accepted on 8 November 2026. Parts 03 and 04 record the agreed weapon
      behaviour, production readiness and ordered implementation scope. The
      remaining integration questions are explicitly identified there.
      Production implementation is separate work; acceptance does not claim
      delivery.
    </p>
  </aside>
  <section>
    <h2>01 / One precise shot, a diminishing reach</h2>
    <p>
      Light Ion Cannon should become a dependable baseline weapon: a directional
      fitting that fires along one straight line and hits immediately. Its
      character is a thin, bright ion ray, leaning blue rather than green. The
      supplied long, faceted barrel, cyan channel and pale side housing are the
      near-final visual reference. Their proportions need to remain convincing
      on a ship, both in the builder and at flight scale.
    </p>
    <p>
      The central tension is immediacy against dissipation. A shot must read as
      one decisive event, while its weakening end communicates limited reach. A
      fading tip and lingering particles may express that loss, but must not
      turn the ray into a travelling projectile or a line dragged by the ship. A
      separate light-effects layer for the brief ray, with residue on the
      existing persistent effects surface, is a hypothesis to compare in motion.
    </p>
    <p>
      This cycle will investigate the shot’s appearance, useful length and
      rhythm together with its mounting on real ships. A 100–200-unit reach and
      one to three shots per second are starting ranges, not decisions. Damage
      must diminish with distance in whole numbers; thresholds and values follow
      the experience rather than determine it. Recoil is optional and initially
      absent. Combat balance, energy cost and final firing controls remain
      adjacent questions; the study must identify the shipbuilder and gameplay
      integration needed before production delivery.
    </p>
    <figure>
      <img
        src="/studies/light-ion-cannon-2026-11-08/concept.png"
        alt="Supplied Light Ion Cannon concept: long dark faceted barrel, bright blue channel and pale cyan side housing"
      >
      <figcaption>
        Supplied cannon concept. The
        <a href="/studies/light-ion-cannon-2026-11-08/concept.svg"
          >original SVG</a
        >
        retains the ship comparisons and authored geometry. No world scale has
        been selected.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>
  <details id="persistence">
    <summary>02.1 / What remains after an instant shot? — B retained</summary>
    <h2>02.1 / What remains after an instant shot?</h2>
    <p>
      All three proposals fire the same complete 150-unit line at once. The
      brightness falls towards a transparent tip; the line never grows forward.
      A removes the ray after an 80 ms flash. B stamps that ray into the
      existing effects surface. C keeps the brief ray separate and deposits only
      scattered light into that surface. B tests whether a continuous afterimage
      gives weight; C tests whether sparse residue preserves the precision of A
      while making dissipation visible.
    </p>
    <IonCannonTiming client:load />
    <p>
      Replay a single shot first, then enable three shots at two per second. The
      time cursor and marked instants expose what survives between shots. B and
      C use the production persistent surface and its shared 0.1-second
      half-life; A and C draw their flash directly. C’s residue is fixed,
      deterministic light, not moving particles. The cannon uses the supplied
      SVG paths at a provisional 24-unit length beside the production Wasp
      renderer. This is an illustrative fitting, not an accepted mount or
      loadout.
    </p>
    <p>
      This stationary timing fixture isolates light persistence. It does not
      simulate hits, damage, energy, recoil or flight, and cannot establish
      drift during turns, camera changes or mounting clearance. The Motion
      instrument currently lacks shot-event comparisons and separate
      transient-light and residue passes; those are proposed capabilities for a
      later flight trial, not changes to its contract. A separate production LFX
      layer remains unselected.
    </p>
    <p>
      At 100 ms after the single shot, A has disappeared, B still reads as a
      continuous line and C leaves separated blue specks. This establishes a
      visible difference in persistence, not a gameplay conclusion.
      <a href="/studies/light-ion-cannon-2026-11-08/after-100ms.png"
        >Retained 100 ms comparison</a
      >
      uses the single-shot default, stationary camera and deterministic residue;
      no randomness or flight inputs are involved.
    </p>
    <p>
      B / Whole ray afterglow is the preferred appearance and the working
      hypothesis for continued exploration. Its continuous dispersal fits the
      game’s FX language. The fading portion may be slightly too bright to read
      as ion dispersal; that is a tuning question, not a rejection of the
      continuous afterimage. Preserve this exact B as the comparison reference.
      The next comparison should reduce the residual brightness while retaining
      the initial strike, reach, timing and colour, so that dimming does not
      quietly change the weapon’s rhythm. Range, cadence, fitting and final
      rendering architecture remain provisional.
    </p>
  </details>

  <details id="afterglow">
    <summary>02.2 / Less light, the same dispersal — 65% selected</summary>
    <h2>02.2 / Less light, the same dispersal</h2>
    <p>
      B’s continuous afterimage is retained. The upper row is the exact 02.1 B
      reference; the next rows reduce residual brightness to 65% and 35%. All
      three share the original strike for its first 80 ms. The dimmer proposals
      then transition over 40 ms to their residual brightness, retaining the
      same blue-to-transparent profile and production surface decay.
    </p>
    <IonCannonTiming afterglow client:load />
    <p>
      The initial view is 100 ms after firing, midway through the brightness
      transition. Compare it with 250 ms, then replay to judge the full event.
      The hypothesis is that a dimmer afterimage can suggest dispersal while
      keeping B’s coherent line. Reduced visibility may also make the apparent
      reach shorter; the physical 150-unit line is identical in every row.
    </p>
    <p>
      This changes only the presentation gain of the accumulated light after the
      strike, not the shared FX half-life or production renderer. The three-shot
      option uses the same timing in every row; a new strike restores full gain,
      including any surviving light from preceding shots. That coupling is a
      limitation of this scene-surface comparison and should be judged during
      replay. There are no moving particles, flight, collisions or random
      inputs. The 65% residual-light variant is selected. The 35% version also
      remained visually viable, but 65% gave the preferred balance.
    </p>
    <p>
      The retained
      <a href="/studies/light-ion-cannon-2026-11-08/afterglow-250ms.png"
        >250 ms capture</a
      >
      records the single-shot comparison at 0.50 s. All three remain continuous,
      with progressively less visible residual light. This is a visual
      observation; the selected 65% variant carries forward with its unchanged
      80 ms strike and 40 ms transition.
    </p>
  </details>

  <details id="reach">
    <summary>02.3 / How far should the line reach? — 200 selected</summary>
    <h2>02.3 / How far should the line reach?</h2>
    <p>
      The selected 65% continuous afterglow now accompanies three reaches: 100,
      150 and 200 world units. All rows use the same Wasp, cannon, scale,
      initial brightness, 80 ms strike, 40 ms transition and shared decay. Only
      the ray length changes. The blue-to-transparent profile stretches with it,
      so each tip disappears at its own endpoint.
    </p>
    <IonCannonTiming ranges client:load />
    <p>
      The shorter proposal tests a compact, close-range character; 150 retains
      the previous reference; 200 tests whether extra reach gives satisfying
      presence or makes this light cannon feel too extensive. Read the common
      50-unit ruler spacing against the identical ships. Replay once, then
      enable the three-shot sequence to see the repeated impression.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is an appearance comparison at a fixed camera and 3 px/unit, not a
      combat trial. Longer rays contain more visible light because their width
      and local brightness stay the same. No damage bands, targets or collisions
      are implied by the rulers; the transparent endpoint is a visual limit, not
      evidence of readable hit range. Flight-scale readability, aiming, mounting
      and fire cadence still need investigation. 200 units is selected: it feels
      fair and remains a local reach rather than an excessive one.
    </p>
    <p>
      The
      <a href="/studies/light-ion-cannon-2026-11-08/reach-30ms.png"
        >retained reach comparison</a
      >
      records the single shot at 0.28 s, 30 ms after firing, with a stationary
      camera and no randomness. It preserves the common scale and the three
      stretched fade profiles for review.
    </p>
  </details>

  <details id="cadence">
    <summary>02.4 / A shot, or a stream? — 3/s selected</summary>
    <h2>02.4 / A shot, or a stream?</h2>
    <p>
      200 units is retained with the selected 65% continuous afterglow. The
      three rows now fire at one, two and three shots per second. Reach, cannon,
      brightness, width, colour and decay are identical. Each train starts at
      0.25 seconds; only the interval between shots changes.
    </p>
    <IonCannonTiming cadence client:load />
    <p>
      Replay to compare the gaps between shots: whether a deliberate pulse feels
      satisfying, and whether the faster train still reads as separate strikes.
      A common 1.5-second firing window produces two, three and five shots
      respectively. The first-strike markers remain available for inspecting the
      shared flash and dispersal. Three shots per second is selected: the faster
      rhythm remains visually effective and supports keeping individual shot
      damage low.
    </p>
    <p>
      This silent, stationary timing study cannot establish aiming difficulty,
      impact satisfaction, resource cost or combat balance. Damage remains
      unspecified; faster firing does not imply greater intended damage per
      second. As in 02.2, each row’s newest strike restores full presentation
      gain for its surviving afterimage before returning to 65%, so
      repeated-light overlap is part of the visual comparison. There is no
      randomness, camera motion, recoil or flight input.
    </p>
    <p>
      The
      <a href="/studies/light-ion-cannon-2026-11-08/cadence-1290ms.png"
        >retained 1.29 s capture</a
      >
      shows all three 40 ms after their coincident strike at 1.25 s. The faster
      train retains more overlapping light. This snapshot records overlap;
      playback is needed to judge rhythm.
    </p>
  </details>

  <details id="flight">
    <summary>02.5 / An afterimage is not the active ray</summary>
    <h2>02.5 / An afterimage is not the active ray</h2>
    <p>
      The all-FX flight trial exposed a separation problem: the world-fixed
      active line looked as though it was no longer coming from the moving ship.
      World-fixed persistence remains suitable for ion dispersal, but the active
      ray needs to follow the muzzle. The all-FX proposal is rejected and its
      interactive example removed.
    </p>
  </details>
  <section id="lfx">
    <h2>02.6 / Hard light above, dispersal behind</h2>
    <p>
      The active ray now lives on a separate LFX canvas above the entire scene.
      It clears every frame and draws the full 200-unit line from the current
      muzzle and heading during each brief flash. Turning and translation carry
      the active ray with the cannon; no active-ray pixels accumulate.
    </p>
    <IonCannonFlight client:load />
    <p>
      Only blue ion dispersal is stamped into the existing persistent FX
      surface, from the current muzzle during the flash. Its emission is reduced
      to 65%, with a softer core and the shared 0.1-second half-life. Once
      stamped, dispersal stays in the world and is reprojected through the
      following camera. LFX is composed last, above the ship and dispersal. This
      separates active light from its fading history rather than dimming both
      together.
    </p>
    <p>
      The selected 200-unit reach and three shots per second remain. The
      five-tick flash is approximately 80 ms at 60 Hz. B3 uses production Wasp
      movement from rest, with four physics substeps per tick and no randomness;
      B1 and B2 offer longer straight burns and turns. The instrument retains
      its 14-second burn selector, graphs and time cursor. Pause before seeking;
      backward seeks replay FX while LFX redraws the selected instant. The
      camera follows position, not heading, at 1 px/unit with a 50-unit grid.
    </p>
    <p>
      This study implements the proposed layer split only in the experiment. The
      fitting remains illustrative. Gravity, engine FX, hits, damage, recoil,
      arena wrapping and zoom jumps are excluded. A future hit must resolve once
      at firing; the moving visible flash must not sweep additional damage. The
      old 40 ms scene-wide dimming transition is replaced by dimmer dispersal at
      emission; its appearance alongside hard light needs fresh review.
    </p>
    <p>
      The
      <a href="/studies/light-ion-cannon-2026-11-08/lfx-active-1300ms.png"
        >retained active-turn view at 1.30 s</a
      >
      records B3 with the separate top-layer ray. Layer clearing and replay
      correctness are checked separately from the visual judgment still needed.
    </p>
    <p>
      The LFX/FX split is accepted: the ray belongs on the fresh topmost LFX
      pass, and dispersal belongs on persistent FX. The improved connection to
      the moving ship is the reason for retaining this separation.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="fitting">
    <h2>02.7 / A cannon mounted on a ship</h2>
    <p>
      The supplied artwork now sits on real weapon-compatible hull mounts of
      Scout, Wasp, Fighter, Corvette and Fiddler. Its side housing is centred on
      the mount, leaving the long barrel forward. The production fitting uses
      the accepted 16-unit flight length. Earlier size comparisons remain in the
      retained captures.
    </p>
    <IonCannonFitting client:load />
    <p>
      The left view uses the builder’s shared Preview and Plan renderer,
      including actual hull occlusion, mount orientation, slot symbols and
      selected-slot treatment. The right views show the same fitting at 0.65
      flight scale and enlarged three times. Select a mount to compare
      placement; the mirror switch fits its geometric counterpart only when a
      compatible one exists. Other hull equipment is omitted to isolate the
      cannon.
    </p>
    <p>
      Preview judges the physical object; Plan judges its slot representation.
      The existing slots, their placement and layering are accepted as given
      with the selected 16-unit cannon. No mount redesign is required. The
      production renderer uses the genuine validated part and shared procedural
      artwork, retaining normal layering and Plan scaling. The Hangar saves and
      launches this fitting.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is a builder composition study rather than a working equipment
      purchase or installation flow. The 16-unit flight length is selected,
      preserving the supplied artwork’s proportions and housing-centred anchor.
      Existing compatible slots remain unchanged. Multiplicity and firing
      coordination are not settled by accepting the slot geometry. Catalogue
      integration, exact muzzle geometry and fitting persistence remain
      implementation and readiness questions.
    </p>
    <p>
      At the initial 24-unit size, Wasp exposes a long barrel while partially
      hiding its housing; Scout’s default compatible mount hides the cannon
      beneath its hull. These are observations of the earlier 24-unit proposal;
      they do not reopen the accepted slots at 16 units. Retained references
      show
      <a href="/studies/light-ion-cannon-2026-11-08/fitting-wasp-24.png"
        >Wasp</a
      >
      and
      <a href="/studies/light-ion-cannon-2026-11-08/fitting-scout-24.png"
        >Scout</a
      >
      in Preview, single mount, fixed camera, with no randomness. Neither is an
      accepted fit.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="production">
    <h2>Production / Playable cannon encounter</h2>
    <p>
      Playtest amendment: automatic acquisition uses a forward rectangle, one
      flight-scale hull width across and 200 units long, rather than the earlier
      centreline. Each funded cannon still casts its own straight muzzle ray and
      can miss.
    </p>
    <p>
      The four implementation slices use the genuine Light Ion Cannon, shared
      artwork and muzzle, production simulation and production FX/LFX. The
      experiments above remain supporting evidence. The fitting above now shows
      the real accepted 16-unit equipment; it no longer uses the rail stand-in.
      Gameplay and automated verification are separate from owner visual and
      balance acceptance.
    </p>
    <IonCannonEncounter client:load />
  </section>
  <section id="readiness">
    <h2>03 / Ready to scope an alpha weapon</h2>
    <p>
      The accepted weapon is a 16-unit fitting on existing compatible slots,
      with a 200-unit instant ray at three shots per second. Four equal 50-unit
      distance bands deal 16, 12, 8 and 4 integer damage per hit. The builder
      displays 12–48 damage/s per cannon. Each actual discharge costs 0.2p;
      sustained firing costs 0.6p/s per cannon.
    </p>
    <p>
      Automatic fire is triggered by an object intersecting the ship’s forward
      centreline within reach. The trigger fires all ready linear weapons. Each
      cannon casts its own straight muzzle line, stops at its first target and
      resolves damage once at discharge. A side-mounted cannon can miss the
      small object that triggered the volley; that is intended. Hits add the
      existing laser-style impact plume. The active ray remains on topmost LFX;
      only dispersal persists on FX.
    </p>
    <p>
      The current salvage system already owns weapon cooldowns, power debit,
      ray/body intersection, first-contact obstruction and damage events. Its
      laser aims independently and only fires after finding a muzzle-line hit;
      the cannon instead needs a shared centreline trigger and must allow paid
      discharges that miss. Existing first-hit and impact-event code is
      reusable, but the new integer falloff must use each muzzle’s actual
      contact distance. The current shared ten-power reserve is an existing
      policy to reconcile explicitly with the new weapon, not an additional
      agreed cannon cost.
    </p>
    <p>
      Production delivery requires a genuine part identifier and Zod contracts,
      shared procedural cannon artwork and muzzle geometry, catalogue
      statistics, loadout persistence, and the LFX pass in the gameplay
      renderer. The study’s rail stand-in and supplied-SVG painter are fixtures,
      not production integration. The existing impact/collision FX provides the
      plume machinery; its activation at the cannon’s actual contact must be
      verified.
    </p>
    <p>
      No further visual exploration blocks scoping. Implementation must define
      exact band-edge conventions, eligible trigger objects and occlusion using
      the game’s real collision geometry, plus deterministic allocation when
      power cannot fund every ready cannon. These are bounded integration
      details, with any consequential departure returned for design review.
      Automated checks must cover centreline triggering, offset misses, first
      contact, band boundaries, power debit, cooldowns and pause/reset; playable
      review must assess hits and LFX attachment separately. Combat balance
      remains provisional until that trial.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="scope">
    <h2>04 / Deliver one complete baseline weapon</h2>
    <p>
      The implementation target is a playable alpha Light Ion Cannon, fitted
      through the existing Hangar and exercised through real encounters. Retain
      the supplied form at 16 world units in flight, existing slots, a 200-unit
      ray, three shots per second, integer 16/12/8/4 damage bands, 0.2p per
      discharge, centreline-triggered automatic volleys, first-target stopping,
      laser-style impact plume, fresh topmost LFX and 65% dispersal on FX.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Design-principle disclosure:</strong>
      no change to
      <code>docs/design.md</code>
      is proposed. This agreed automatic weapon adds no firing button and does
      not retune movement. The scope was accepted on 8 November 2026; production
      implementation is separate work.
    </p>

    <h3>1 / Equipment and shared geometry — medium</h3>
    <p>
      Add a genuine Light Ion Cannon part ID and validated weapon definition
      before changing consumers. Define the four damage bands, rate, energy per
      shot and range together, rather than deriving gameplay from builder
      strings. Convert the supplied paths to shared procedural Canvas artwork;
      preserve their silhouette, colours, proportions and housing-centred mount
      anchor. Derive the muzzle from that same geometry. A 16-unit flight extent
      corresponds to the shared 0.65 hull-to-flight transform, not a second
      independently scaled drawing.
    </p>
    <p>
      Owners: mode-a loadout and salvage Zod schemas, part catalogue, build
      renderer and weapon-pose geometry; shared artwork belongs in the
      appropriate reusable drawing owner. Add a cannon contract under
      <code>docs/specs/game/</code>
      alongside its implementation. Deliver an inspectable real fitting in the
      canonical equipment Lab and this study. Check compatible and incompatible
      slots, geometry/muzzle agreement, existing saved-loadout parsing and
      unchanged existing equipment. No new dependency is needed.
    </p>

    <h3>2 / Centreline-triggered instant volleys — large</h3>
    <p>
      Depends on slice 1. Extend the simulation-owned weapon system with one
      ship-centreline detection result per firing update. Snapshot the eligible
      ready cannons for a volley before resolving its hits, so destruction by
      the first cannon cannot cancel the remaining volley. Each funded cannon
      fires along its own muzzle line and pays 0.2p even when that line misses.
      Resolve the nearest actual contact, apply one integer damage amount by
      muzzle-to-contact distance, and start its one-third-second cooldown. Empty
      centreline means no automatic discharge. Do not inherit rail recoil or
      laser tracking.
    </p>
    <p>
      Owners: salvage simulation, collision helpers and weapon-event schema. The
      new cannon contract must specify exact edges at 50, 100, 150 and 200
      units; intended default is the lower-damage band at each internal boundary
      and inclusion of the 200-unit endpoint. Reuse current damageable encounter
      objects and world obstruction geometry; exclude collectible energy from
      triggering. Confirm those integration defaults, the existing ten-power
      reserve and insufficient-volley-power allocation before implementing them
      if they change intended behaviour. Do not silently give other weapons the
      new trigger policy.
    </p>
    <p>
      Evidence: deterministic tests for the four bands and their boundaries,
      first-target blocking, no penetration, centreline activation, side-mounted
      misses, all-ready volley behaviour when the first hit destroys the
      trigger, per-cannon cooldowns, paid misses, low-power handling and
      effects-disabled gameplay equivalence. This slice can deliver simulation
      correctness before final visual integration.
    </p>

    <h3>3 / Production hard light, dispersal and impact — medium to large</h3>
    <p>
      Depends on slices 1 and 2. Add a bounded, fresh LFX pass above the game
      scene, shared by play and the mixed-effects Lab. Active rays use the
      current muzzle and heading for the approximately 80 ms visible flash;
      damage stays tied to the single discharge event. Paint only blue dispersal
      into the persistent FX surface at the selected 65% emission treatment.
      Reuse the existing impact plume at the actual first contact. Keep the
      accepted transparent tip and blue ion identity.
    </p>
    <p>
      Owners: FlightFx, salvage renderer, FlightDeck and the mixed-effects
      bench; update <code>docs/specs/factory/effects/spec.md</code> with LFX
      ordering, bounded work and lifecycle. Separate hit data from current
      presentation state. How a muzzle-following flash terminates against a
      moving hit target needs an inspectable hit trial: it must not show
      penetration or sweep fresh damage while turning. Treat an unresolved
      result as a focused return to exploration, not permission to alter hit
      semantics.
    </p>
    <p>
      Check empty LFX between flashes, muzzle attachment during movement and
      turns, dispersal remaining in world space, pause without accumulation,
      reset, wrap without bridging, camera pan and zoom. Exercise sustained
      multiple cannons alongside engines and impacts in the mixed Lab, measuring
      against the existing effects budget. Passing lifecycle tests does not
      establish visual fidelity or performance acceptance.
    </p>

    <h3>4 / Hangar delivery and playable review — medium</h3>
    <p>
      Catalogue and persistence work can follow slice 1; end-to-end review
      depends on slices 2 and 3. Offer the real cannon through existing
      compatible slots and both Preview/Plan views, without moving slots. Show
      <strong>12–48 damage/s</strong>
      per cannon, three shots/s, 200-unit reach and <strong>0.2p/shot</strong>;
      0.6p/s is the continuous-fire equivalent, not an idle drain. Preserve
      keyboard and landscape touch flight controls. Retain the intended low
      per-hit damage rather than rebalancing before play.
    </p>
    <p>
      Owners: ShipBuilder and its catalogue, loadout storage/handoff, title and
      flight consumers. Update <code>docs/specs/game/hangar/spec.md</code> and
      the cannon contract with actual delivered behaviour. Verify installation,
      removal, multiple fittings, save/reload and launch handoff on real ships.
      Keep existing outfits compatible; changing starter loadouts is outside
      this slice unless explicitly agreed.
    </p>
    <p>
      Finish with a playable small-target encounter demonstrating deliberate
      side-cannon misses, a close and distant hit, a stopped ray and plume, and
      a moving/turning volley. Inspect the cannon at builder and flight scale.
      Run affected unit, browser, type, contract and build gates required by
      development guidance; report failures and unavailable checks separately.
      Human review judges connection, rhythm, impact and balance independently.
    </p>

    <h3>Delivery boundary</h3>
    <p>
      These are four reviewable slices, not four independent releases. Geometry
      enables simulation and catalogue work; simulation enables real LFX/impact
      events; the final encounter joins them. The largest uncertainty is
      production layer lifecycle and hit presentation during turns, followed by
      volley power policy. Sizes are relative implementation estimates,
      excluding design-review waiting time. No multiplayer, new weapon families,
      gravity/friction changes, mount redesign, paid services or publication is
      included. Production behaviour remains unchanged by this scope document.
    </p>
  </section>
</StudyLayout>

<style>
section > p {
  max-width: 72ch;
}
figure {
  margin: var(--spacing-lg) 0;
}
img {
  display: block;
  width: auto;
  max-width: 100%;
  height: 420px;
  object-fit: contain;
  background: var(--color-void);
}
figcaption {
  margin-top: var(--spacing-md);
  max-width: 72ch;
  color: var(--color-text-muted);
}
</style>
